ction?","options":{"O1":"Run a causal intervention, such as inserting unrelated or shuffled reasoning content, before attributing the effect to reasoning, and state the data does not isolate the mechanism.","O2":"Headline that most of the reduction comes from format and structure, since stripped-reasoning data captures the bulk of the drop, with reasoning content adding a smaller benefit.","O3":"Report only that reasoning data lowers misalignment in this model and the weaker second model, and flag that the stripped-format control leaves the mechanism unresolved.","O4":"State that the results are consistent with the hypothesis, say the format-only version also has some effect, and describe the full trace as giving a further benefit.","O5":"Run an additional ablation with reasoning disabled and compare effect sizes across conditions, to argue that the effect is not wholly attributable to explicit reasoning."}},{"key":"f800801a91a9","context":"The paper studies mitigations for broad misalignment from narrow bad finetuning that are used in production post-training: mixing in benign data, training-time instructions that frame bad behaviour as sanctioned, and on-policy training. Earlier work also evaluated KL-divergence regularization and preventative activation steering with an evil-persona vector. The team had to decide whether to test if these also leave misalignment hidden behind specific triggers.","question":"Should the team test additional defenses from earlier work for hidden trigger-dependent misalignment?","options":{"O1":"Add KL regularization as a cheap matched baseline, since it is simple to run and tests whether any in-training constraint hides rather than removes misalignment.","O2":"Skip the other defenses, state that conclusions apply only to the studied mitigations, and put trigger-dependence checks for the others in a limitations section.","O3":"Extend the study to KL regularization and activation steering, testing whether each hides misalignment behind